$OctEightArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-yogurt-cup-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Yogurt Cup Labels: Fit the Lid, Chill and Flavor Change'
    Description = 'Plan custom yogurt cup labels around lid diameter, curved cup walls, cold storage, flavor variants and readable food information.'
    Category = 'Chilled food packaging'
    CardDescription = 'A good cup label fits the filled pack after refrigeration, not just the flat artwork.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of chilled yogurt cups with complete original strawberry, peach and blueberry illustrated lid and side labels'
    Caption = 'Check lid placement and side-panel readability on filled, chilled cups.'
    Published = '2026-10-08'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can the same label fit every yogurt cup?', 'No. Measure the actual lid and sidewall of each cup, including any taper, rim and seal area, before approving artwork.'),
      @('Does a film label automatically withstand condensation?', 'No. Face stock, adhesive, finish, application conditions and the cup surface all matter. Trial the proposed construction on filled cups through the real cold chain.'),
      @('Who approves nutrition and ingredient wording?', 'The food brand is responsible for market-specific mandatory information and claims. The printer can review layout and legibility but should not invent approved copy.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>On a flat proof, the strawberry cup and peach cup can look like twins with different colors. On a refrigerated shelf, the useful differences are harder: can someone read the flavor from the front, does the lid label sit inside the sealing rim, and does the side label stay put when the cup comes out of the chiller?</p>
  <p>I would begin with three physical pieces: the final cup, its sealed lid and one filled sample at serving temperature. Nominal volume is not a dieline. A slightly tapered wall, a raised rim or a different foil lid can turn a perfectly centered drawing into a crooked applied label.</p>
  <h2>Decide What the Lid and Wall Each Need to Say</h2>
  <p>A lid sticker may carry the flavor and original fruit illustration. The side panel may need more room for product name, ingredients, nutrition, date code and the approved barcode. Do not crowd mandatory information onto a tiny round lid just because it photographs well. Let the food brand confirm which text is required in each destination market; the label supplier should reserve space and flag type that becomes unreadable at print size.</p>
  <p>Make the fruit art genuinely useful. A full berry illustration and a full peach illustration help a packing team spot a mixed sleeve without relying on color alone. Pair that visual cue with a readable flavor word and the correct approved SKU code. For a multi-flavor run, a small proof matrix is more dependable than one generic mockup.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Label After Filling and Chilling</h2>
  <p>Where is the label applied: on dry cups before filling, on sealed cups at room temperature, or after refrigeration? Those are different tests. Label representative filled cups in the planned sequence. Check edge lift, scuffing, condensation and how the printed flavor reads through a chilled display case. A material advertised as moisture resistant is a starting candidate, not a promise about this specific cup.</p>
  <p>Watch the lid seal area closely. A decorative sticker should not interfere with the closure or the consumer's opening motion. If the lid is printed directly, compare the economics and flexibility of direct print with an applied label instead of assuming one method is always better.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send final cup, lid and filled sample</li><li>Measure usable top and side panels</li><li>Confirm application temperature and sequence</li><li>Proof each flavor, code and date area</li><li>Inspect refrigerated and handled samples</li><li>Have the brand approve all food copy</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/cold-brew-bottle-labels-guide/">cold brew bottle guide</a> covers another chilled-pack problem, while the <a href="/blog/retail-barcode-labels-for-product-packaging/">retail barcode guide</a> helps protect the scan area. Send cup photos, flavor count and the label application plan through the <a href="/get-quote/">quote form</a>. The right label is the one that still makes sense on a cold, finished cup.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'board-game-component-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Board Game Component Stickers: Cut, Sort and Pack Without Confusion'
    Description = 'Plan custom board game sticker sheets and tokens around artwork versions, cutlines, peel order, component counts and box packing.'
    Category = 'Game component production'
    CardDescription = 'The artwork is only half the job; the right pieces must reach the right box.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original fantasy garden board game sticker sheets with complete illustrated tokens being sorted beside an unbranded game box'
    Caption = 'A component count sheet belongs beside the printed sticker proof.'
    Published = '2026-10-08'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should board game stickers be supplied as sheets or loose pieces?', 'Choose from the assembly workflow. Sheets help count and pack a set; loose die-cut pieces may suit a different player or packing experience.'),
      @('Can small token text be read after cutting?', 'Only a full-size printed proof can show that reliably. Leave space around the cutline and check the smallest text on the actual piece.'),
      @('Who checks game age and safety statements?', 'The game publisher or authorized product owner must review target-market requirements and approve all statements. Do not infer compliance from the sticker material alone.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A game can have beautiful illustrated tokens and still ship with the wrong number of them. One sheet has six flower markers; the packing list expects eight. A new artwork version fixes the flower but leaves the old number on the back. I would put the component list next to the visual proof before discussing gloss or matte.</p>
  <p>First decide whether players peel from a sheet, receive loose die-cut stickers, or apply permanent graphics during assembly. The answer changes cut spacing, liner choice, sorting and packing. A sticker sheet that looks tidy in a render can be awkward when a player tries to lift the smallest icon with a fingernail.</p>
  <h2>Design a Countable Sheet</h2>
  <p>Give every faction, color and token type a short name in the production file. Count pieces per game, per sheet and per carton. If artwork is revised, keep the sheet version tied to the rulebook and box version. A simple two-column checklist often catches more trouble than another round of cosmetic layout changes.</p>
  <p>Use original, complete artwork: for example a garden game might have illustrated mushrooms, lanterns, leaves and tiny map symbols. The drawing should remain recognizable after the actual cut. Avoid borrowing a known game's characters or icons. A publisher-supplied design should be licensed or owned by the customer.</p>
  <h2>Proof at Playing Size</h2>
  <p>Print a full-size sample and ask someone unfamiliar with the file to identify every symbol. Check whether the cutline clips a face, whether adjacent kiss cuts leave enough finger room, and whether the sheet fits the box insert. Test peeling without tearing the printed face or lifting the neighboring piece.</p>
  <p>If children are the intended users, have the product owner review age grading, materials and labeling for the target market. A friendly illustration is not a safety assessment, and a printer should not add a compliance badge without documentation.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide component count per finished game</li><li>Identify sheet versus loose-piece packing</li><li>Send final-size artwork and cutlines</li><li>Check peel access for the smallest token</li><li>Match proof, rules and box versions</li><li>Approve final packed-set count</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/products/custom-sticker-sheets/">custom sticker sheets</a> page shows the format options, and the <a href="/blog/sticker-artwork-file-formats/">artwork file guide</a> explains what to send. Use the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a> to describe the game box, sheet size and component count. A good print run is not finished until the pieces are in the right pack.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-bookplate-stickers-for-publishers'
    Title = 'Custom Bookplate Stickers: Make the Gift Feel Personal Without Damaging the Book'
    Description = 'Plan custom bookplate stickers for publishers and booksellers with original art, writing space, paper feel, binding clearance and removal expectations.'
    Category = 'Publishing and stationery'
    CardDescription = 'The writing space and the book paper matter as much as the illustration.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original botanical bookplate stickers with complete printed designs and handwritten names inside unbranded hardcover books'
    Caption = 'Check the plate on the actual endpaper, with a real name written in the blank field.'
    Published = '2026-10-08'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will a bookplate sticker remove cleanly later?', 'Do not promise clean removal without testing the actual paper, coating and dwell time. Some endpapers can tear or stain when a label is lifted.'),
      @('Can customers write on coated bookplate stickers?', 'It depends on the finish and pen. Test the intended pen on the printed sample, including drying and smudge resistance.'),
      @('Where should a bookplate be applied?', 'Publishers often choose an endpaper or flyleaf, but the correct position depends on binding, artwork and archival expectations. Test placement on the finished book.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A bookplate has one small job that a decorative sticker does not: it asks a person to write a name. In the mockup, the blank rectangle can look generous. In a real hardcover, a long surname hits the floral border and the pen smears on the finish. I would test the handwriting before approving the ornament.</p>
  <p>For a publisher, bookstore or gift-box brand, the plate can make a limited edition feel considered without printing a different book block. But the label must live on a page people will touch, not on a product pack that gets thrown away. Endpaper texture, binding clearance and adhesive choice deserve a real sample.</p>
  <h2>Give the Name More Room Than You Think</h2>
  <p>Print a few complete original designs, such as a small botanical frame, a library shelf or a night-sky motif. Leave a calm writing area inside each. Write short and long names with the pen buyers are likely to use. If the ink beads, smears or disappears into a dark illustration, choose a different finish or more open layout.</p>
  <p>Keep the words purposeful: "This book belongs to" can work when the audience is clear, but a gifting edition may need space for both giver and recipient. Do not print a fake library seal or imitate a famous publisher's mark. The brand can use its own approved logo and original artwork.</p>
  <h2>Test the Finished Book, Not Loose Paper</h2>
  <p>Place the sticker on the actual flyleaf or endpaper. Open and close the book several times. Check whether the plate catches at the gutter or shows through a thin page. If it is intended to be removable, trial removal after a realistic dwell; do not claim it will lift cleanly from every book paper. If it should remain for years, ask the product owner what archival expectations apply before choosing a construction.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send an actual finished book sample</li><li>Confirm plate position and usable size</li><li>Test the intended pen and longest name</li><li>Review endpaper texture and show-through</li><li>Define permanence or removal expectations</li><li>Check batch packing by title or edition</li></ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/products/custom-stationery-stickers/">stationery sticker category</a> for related formats and the <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">removable versus permanent guide</a> for the adhesive conversation. A clear photo of the book's endpaper and the finished plate size will make a <a href="/get-quote/">quote request</a> far more useful.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'labels-for-powder-coated-metal-equipment'
    Title = 'Labels for Powder-Coated Metal Equipment: Test the Surface First'
    Description = 'Plan custom equipment labels on powder-coated metal with surface texture, cleaning, edges, serial data and applied-sample checks.'
    Category = 'Industrial equipment labeling'
    CardDescription = 'A smooth drawing does not show the texture or cleaning routine of the real panel.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of powder-coated metal equipment panels bearing complete original illustrated operating labels and serial fields'
    Caption = 'Apply test labels to the finished coated panel, not bare metal.'
    Published = '2026-10-08'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will a label that sticks to bare metal also stick to powder coat?', 'Not necessarily. The cured coating changes texture and surface behavior. Test on a finished production panel.'),
      @('Can a label replace a required safety plate?', 'That depends on the product and applicable requirements. The equipment owner must approve the labeling method and content; do not substitute a decorative sticker for a required durable marking.'),
      @('When should serial numbers be added?', 'Decide the data source, print method and inspection step before artwork approval. Trial scan and legibility on the final installed panel.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>An equipment panel can be beautifully coated and surprisingly difficult to label. The artwork proof shows a crisp operating icon, but the real powder coat has texture, an edge radius and a cleaner used every Friday. I would ask for a finished spare panel before recommending a label construction.</p>
  <p>Separate three jobs: product identification, operating instructions and any required safety marking. They may need different sizes, materials or approval routes. A printer can help reproduce a supplied warning layout; the equipment owner must determine whether the wording, placement and durability meet the relevant rules.</p>
  <h2>Find the Actual Landing Area</h2>
  <p>Measure the flat area after coating and assembly. Keep the label away from screws, vents, hot zones and sharp bends unless the product design explicitly allows it. Note whether operators will read it standing over the machine or reaching around the side. A complete original diagram of a control position can help, but only when it matches the equipment owner's approved instruction.</p>
  <p>Texture and cleaning chemistry can make one panel behave differently from another. Do not choose adhesive from a generic phrase such as "industrial strength." Apply candidate labels to cured production panels using the planned surface preparation and pressure. Observe them after the owner's normal cleaner, handling and temperature cycle. A short bench trial is not a certified lifetime claim.</p>
  <h2>Make Data and Inspection Part of the Artwork</h2>
  <p>If each unit needs a serial number or QR code, reserve a clean field and decide who supplies the data. Print the smallest expected characters at final size. Install the label, then scan and read it from the operator's actual angle. Version the artwork so a revised instruction does not accidentally ship on an older panel.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply a finished powder-coated panel</li><li>Mark flat area, edges and installation angle</li><li>List cleaners and handling conditions</li><li>Provide owner-approved warnings and data</li><li>Trial application, reading and scanning</li><li>Keep proof revision with the equipment version</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-electronics-serial-number-labels-guide/">serial number label guide</a> covers data control, and the <a href="/blog/sticker-adhesive-surface-matching-guide/">adhesive surface guide</a> frames the material test. Share a coated-panel photo and the label's purpose through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The important sample is the one stuck to the metal the buyer actually ships.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-hotel-amenity-bottle-labels'
    Title = 'Hotel Amenity Bottle Labels: Make Small Type Work in a Wet Bathroom'
    Description = 'Plan custom hotel amenity bottle labels for shampoo, conditioner and lotion with small-pack readability, wet handling, variant control and refill workflows.'
    Category = 'Hospitality amenity packaging'
    CardDescription = 'Guests need to find shampoo quickly; housekeeping needs the right bottle in the right room.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original botanical hotel amenity labels on small unbranded shampoo conditioner and lotion bottles in a real bathroom setting'
    Caption = 'Check the bottle set at bathroom distance with wet hands and ordinary lighting.'
    Published = '2026-10-08'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can shampoo and lotion use one shared label?', 'A shared visual system is useful, but each product still needs its own correct name and owner-approved content. Proof and pack variants separately.'),
      @('Are waterproof labels required for every hotel bottle?', 'Not by name alone. Define shower exposure, cleaning, dispensing and refill conditions, then test the proposed construction on the actual bottle.'),
      @('Who checks cosmetic ingredient and claim rules?', 'The product brand or responsible distributor must approve market-specific wording and claims. The label supplier can flag space and readability concerns.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>In a hotel bathroom, a guest does not study a label like a buyer studies a catalog. They reach for a small bottle with wet hands, perhaps before their glasses are on. If shampoo and lotion are indistinguishable until the bottle is inches from the face, the elegant label has failed its first task.</p>
  <p>I would line up the actual amenity set on the bathroom shelf and look at it from the shower and sink. The bottle family might share one original botanical illustration, but each product needs a strong, readable name and an unmistakable secondary cue. Housekeeping also needs to refill or replace the right item without slowing the room turn.</p>
  <h2>Design a System, Then Proof Each Bottle</h2>
  <p>Give shampoo, conditioner, body wash and lotion a consistent brand frame while changing a clear word and visual detail for each. An original leaf, seed or citrus design can be complete and attractive without copying another hotel's mark. Keep the longest product name at actual print size. The label on a 30 mL bottle will not behave like the same design enlarged on a dispenser.</p>
  <p>Measure both the travel-size bottle and any refillable wall-mounted version. Curves, pump shoulders and viewing angle change the useful print panel. The responsible product owner must approve ingredient copy, warnings and claims for every market; a printer should not invent a generic cosmetic ingredient list to fill space.</p>
  <h2>Test Wet Use and the Cleaning Routine</h2>
  <p>Apply samples to the final bottle material. Handle them with wet hands, expose them to the actual formula where spills occur, and include the property's normal cleaning method in the trial. Check print rub, edge lift and whether a transparent label disappears against a pale liquid. If the amenity is refilled, decide whether the bottle label should stay, be replaced or carry variable batch information.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send every final bottle and dispenser size</li><li>Confirm product names and variant count</li><li>Test readability at bathroom distance</li><li>Check wet handling and cleaning</li><li>Approve market-specific product copy</li><li>Define refill and replacement workflow</li></ul>
  <p>Use the <a href="/blog/custom-shampoo-bottle-labels-guide/">shampoo bottle guide</a> for wider cosmetic packaging questions and the <a href="/blog/waterproof-custom-stickers-buying-guide/">waterproof sticker guide</a> for test planning. A photo of the full bottle set and its intended bathroom position belongs with the <a href="/get-quote/">quote request</a>. The label should help a guest choose correctly before they have to squint.</p>
'@
